{
  "case_id": "2020-19-1257",
  "document_hash": "55b2508ae388309d74b45f6641632ad6ab468797e777622073ce852eeb5b0c3e",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "MICHAEL A. CARVIN",
      "case_id": "2020-19-1257",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "55b2508ae388309d74b45f6641632ad6ab468797e777622073ce852eeb5b0c3e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-55b2508ae388-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I think the key conceptual point here to understand is that Arizona has not denied anyone any voting opportunity of any kind. There's not, like, a literacy test which denies you the right to vote. It's not like vote dilution, where white bloc voting denies minorities an equal opportunity to elect."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MICHAEL A. CARVIN",
      "case_id": "2020-19-1257",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "55b2508ae388309d74b45f6641632ad6ab468797e777622073ce852eeb5b0c3e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-55b2508ae388-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "contexts. Under Title VII, disparate impact relates to a denial of an employment opportunity, a job or a promotion. It doesn't get involved in the process. No one's ever brought a Title VII claim saying you can't require people to send in applications because minorities have less access to transportation and mail, analogous to the claim being made here."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MICHAEL A. CARVIN",
      "case_id": "2020-19-1257",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "55b2508ae388309d74b45f6641632ad6ab468797e777622073ce852eeb5b0c3e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-55b2508ae388-0000-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 7,
        "start": 7
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Respondents' alternative view is at war with the text of Section 2. Section 2 says, again, voting practices cannot provide less opportunity. They say that voting practices which provide the same opportunity are nonetheless unlawful if external socioeconomic factors somehow contribute to disproportionate utilization."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2020-19-1257",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "55b2508ae388309d74b45f6641632ad6ab468797e777622073ce852eeb5b0c3e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-55b2508ae388-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 10,
        "start": 10
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE THOMAS: Thank you, Mr. Chief Justice. Mr. Carvin, I -- I under -- your -- I understand your race neutrality argument, and normally you see that in -- to come in -- in -- in the context of a non-discrimination statute or Fourteenth Amendment that under -- that really requires equal treatment. How does that race neutrality approach fit within the language of the Voting Rights Act, though, that doesn't speak in -- in those terms?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2020-19-1257",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "55b2508ae388309d74b45f6641632ad6ab468797e777622073ce852eeb5b0c3e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-55b2508ae388-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Justice KAGAN: Mr. Carvin, I have a number of hypotheticals for you, and I'd -- I'd be grateful if we could run through these fairly quickly just so I can get an understanding of your position.\nSo the first one is that the state decides that each county can have one polling place, and because of who lives in -- in -- in Heritage Reporting Corporation"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner (Arizona)",
      "case_id": "2020-19-1257",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "55b2508ae388309d74b45f6641632ad6ab468797e777622073ce852eeb5b0c3e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-55b2508ae388-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 25
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. CARVIN: I would say not.\n\"Equally open\" means takes into account demographic realities. If you have one polling place for five people and one polling place for 5 million people, obviously, in the latter situation, those people do not have an equal opportunity to vote."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Barrett",
      "case_id": "2020-19-1257",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "55b2508ae388309d74b45f6641632ad6ab468797e777622073ce852eeb5b0c3e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-55b2508ae388-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 36,
        "start": 36
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BARRETT: Okay. But then I don't understand why you conceded in your examples to Justice Kagan that some of those time, place, and manner restrictions -- like time, place, and manner, you can only vote at a country club, or time, place, and manner, you know, this is the placement of the polls and they're going to be placed in areas that are burdensome to minorities. Aren't those time, place, and manner restrictions?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2020-19-1257",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "55b2508ae388309d74b45f6641632ad6ab468797e777622073ce852eeb5b0c3e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-55b2508ae388-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE THOMAS: The -- there was a -- the theory that the Ninth Circuit used to discuss some questionable legislative intent"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Brnovich",
      "case_id": "2020-19-1257",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "55b2508ae388309d74b45f6641632ad6ab468797e777622073ce852eeb5b0c3e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-55b2508ae388-0002-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 45,
        "start": 45
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. BRNOVICH: Justice Thomas, I --we believe that the cat's paw doctrine is completely inapplicable to a case like that."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2020-19-1257",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "55b2508ae388309d74b45f6641632ad6ab468797e777622073ce852eeb5b0c3e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-55b2508ae388-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 48,
        "start": 48
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: Can I ask you something about the statistics regarding out-of-precinct voting. Are -- do they refer only to voters who cast their ballots at a polling place on Election Day, or do they also include voters who voted early?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Heritage Reporting Corporation",
      "case_id": "2020-19-1257",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "55b2508ae388309d74b45f6641632ad6ab468797e777622073ce852eeb5b0c3e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-55b2508ae388-0003-0000",
      "kind": "allegation",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 68,
        "start": 68
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. AMUNSON: Your Honor, what I'm saying is that, here, what we have is a record that shows that Native Americans and Latinos in Arizona rely disproportionately on ballot collection and white voters do not. So that is why this is before you."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2020-19-1257",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "55b2508ae388309d74b45f6641632ad6ab468797e777622073ce852eeb5b0c3e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-55b2508ae388-0003-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 70,
        "start": 70
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE THOMAS: And there's --and the Secretary of State plans to enforce it?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2020-19-1257",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "55b2508ae388309d74b45f6641632ad6ab468797e777622073ce852eeb5b0c3e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-55b2508ae388-0003-0003",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 70,
        "start": 70
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the out-of-precinct policy was enforced in the 2020 election."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Gorsuch",
      "case_id": "2020-19-1257",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "55b2508ae388309d74b45f6641632ad6ab468797e777622073ce852eeb5b0c3e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-55b2508ae388-0004-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 86,
        "start": 86
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GORSUCH: I'm afraid that -- yeah, I'm just asking, you know, how many elections? What -- what would be enough in -- in -- in the Secretary's view?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Amunson",
      "case_id": "2020-19-1257",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "55b2508ae388309d74b45f6641632ad6ab468797e777622073ce852eeb5b0c3e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-55b2508ae388-0004-0006",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 87,
        "start": 87
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I am saying that criminalizing non-fraudulent ballot collection simply is -- does not get at the state's interest in preventing fraud."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2020-19-1257",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "55b2508ae388309d74b45f6641632ad6ab468797e777622073ce852eeb5b0c3e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-55b2508ae388-0004-0005",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 90,
        "start": 90
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Arizona had a 25-year history of literally not a single instance of fraud with ballot collection."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Secretary Hobbs",
      "case_id": "2020-19-1257",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "55b2508ae388309d74b45f6641632ad6ab468797e777622073ce852eeb5b0c3e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-55b2508ae388-0004-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 95,
        "start": 95
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Secretary Hobbs submits that the real injury here is to the Native American, Latino, and black citizens of Arizona whose right to vote has been denied or abridged by the out-of-precinct policy and the criminalization of neighbors helping neighbors deliver their ballots."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondents DNC",
      "case_id": "2020-19-1257",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "55b2508ae388309d74b45f6641632ad6ab468797e777622073ce852eeb5b0c3e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-55b2508ae388-0004-0007",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 96,
        "start": 96
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The Ninth Circuit applied the correct test to determine that Arizona's policy of entirely disenfranchising voters who cast out-of-precinct ballots and its criminal ban on non-fraudulent ballot collection violates Section 2 of the Voting Rights Act."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2020-19-1257",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "55b2508ae388309d74b45f6641632ad6ab468797e777622073ce852eeb5b0c3e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-55b2508ae388-0005-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 106,
        "start": 106
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: All right. So answer -- tell me how those factors get considered in your -- in your views."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2020-19-1257",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "55b2508ae388309d74b45f6641632ad6ab468797e777622073ce852eeb5b0c3e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-55b2508ae388-0005-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 113,
        "start": 113
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. SPIVA: Well, two things, Your Honor. The Carter- Baker Report was not something that the legislature here considered. And -- and even the recommendations of the Carter-Baker Report was not based on any evidence of -- of -- of ballot collection fraud anywhere in the country. The legislature -- and the district court found this -- had no evidence of voter fraud, not only in Arizona, but anywhere in the country at the time that it passed the criminal ballot collection ban."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2020-19-1257",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "55b2508ae388309d74b45f6641632ad6ab468797e777622073ce852eeb5b0c3e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-55b2508ae388-0005-0003",
      "kind": "stated_consequence",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 117,
        "start": 117
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "This Court said in Shelby County that Section 2 remained as a permanent nationwide ban on voting discrimination, and the Court acknowledged that voting discrimination still exists. No one doubts this. This is proven not just an accurate description of the times in 2013 but also prophetic. More voting restrictions have been enacted over the last decade than at any point since the end of Jim Crow."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2020-19-1257",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "55b2508ae388309d74b45f6641632ad6ab468797e777622073ce852eeb5b0c3e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-55b2508ae388-0005-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 121,
        "start": 121
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: Thank you, counsel. The case is submitted."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2020-19-1257",
      "confidence": 0.0,
